'''Juan Arévalo''' was an Assembly Member of the Declaration of Philippine Independence, which was proclaimed in 1898.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bautista |first1=Ambrosio Rianzares |title=Declaration of Independence |url=https://thecorpusjuris.com/constitutions/declaration-of-independence.php |website=The Corpus Juris |access-date=11 February 2022 |language=en |date=12 June 1898}}</ref> He was the son of [[Bonifacio Flores Arévalo]] (founding president of the ''Sociedad Dental de Filipinas'') and Trinidad Arévalo. His remains are buried with other Assembly Members of the [[Philippine Declaration of Independence|Declaration of Philippine Independence]], at the Mausoleo de los Veteranos de la Revolución in [[Manila North Cemetery]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Mausoleum of Heroes |url=https://www.pressreader.com/philippines/manila-times/20190826/281582357290774 |access-date=11 February 2022 |via=PressReader |publisher=The Manila Times |date=26 August 2019}}</ref>

