'''John Casimir, Count Palatine of Zweibrücken-Kleeburg''' ({{langx|de|Johann Kasimir}}; {{langx|sv|Johan Kasimir}}; 20 April 1589, [[Zweibrücken]] &ndash; 18 June 1652, [[Stegeborg Castle]]) was the son of [[John I, Count Palatine of Zweibrücken]] and his wife, Duchess [[Magdalene of Jülich-Cleves-Berg]]. He was married to [[Catherine of Sweden, Countess Palatine of Kleeburg|Catherine of Sweden]] and was the founder of a branch of [[House of Wittelsbach|Wittelsbach]] [[Count palatine|Counts Palatine]] often called the ''[[House of Palatinate-Zweibrücken|Swedish line]]'', because it gave rise to three subsequent [[List of Swedish monarchs|kings of Sweden]],<ref name="tomeIV">Michel Huberty, Alain Giraud, F. and B. Magdelaine. ''L'Allemagne Dynastique, Tome IV: Wittelsbach''. (1985). {{ISBN|2-901138-04-7}} pp. 83–84, 108–109, 144–145.</ref> but more commonly known as the Kleeburg (or Cleebourg) line.<ref name="tomeIV"/>

==Biography==
In 1591 his father stipulated that, as the youngest son, John Casimir would receive as [[appanage]] the countship of Neukastell in the [[Palatinate-Zweibrücken|Palatinate]]. Upon their father's death in 1611, however, the eldest son, [[John II, Count Palatine of Zweibrucken]], instead signed a compromise with John Casimir whereby the latter received only the castle at Neukastell coupled with an [[Pension|annuity]] of 3000 [[Florin (Italian coin)|florins]] from the countship's revenues (similarly, John Casimir's elder brother, [[Frederick Casimir, Count Palatine of Zweibrucken-Landsberg|Frederick Casimir]], received the castle at Landsberg with a small surrounding [[Territory (administrative division)|domain]], instead of the entire Landsberg appanage bequeathed to him paternally).<ref name="tomeIV"/>

On 11 June 1615, Casimir married his second cousin [[Catherine of Sweden, Countess Palatine of Kleeburg|Catherine of Sweden]], and their son eventually became King [[Charles X Gustav of Sweden]]. After the outbreak of the [[Thirty Years' War]], John Casimir and his family were forced to flee to [[Strasbourg]] in 1620. In 1622, King [[Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden]], Catherine's half-brother, summoned the family to Sweden. They arrived in late July that year, partly as a result of the dangers posed by the war and the king's lack of surviving siblings and children. The family settled at [[Stegeborg Castle]] in [[Östergötland]], which was granted to John Casimir as security for Catherine's unpaid dowry. He remained in Sweden for the rest of his life and became involved in the country's military administration. From 1625, he generally commanded the forces stationed in Sweden while Gustavus Adolphus campaigned abroad.<ref>{{cite web |title=Johan Kasimir |url=https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=12105 |website=Svenskt biografiskt lexikon |language=sv}}</ref><ref>{{cite encyclopedia |last=Kromnow |first=Åke |title=Johann Casimir |encyclopedia=Neue Deutsche Biographie |volume=10 |year=1974 |pages=515–516 |url=https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/gnd119314118.html#ndbcontent |language=de}}</ref>

== Family ==
Five of his children with Catherine survived infancy:

* [[Christina Magdalena of Pfalz-Zweibrücken|Christina Magdalena]] (1616&ndash;1662); married [[Frederick VI, Margrave of Baden-Durlach]]. [[Adolf Frederick of Sweden]] was her great-grandson.
* [[Charles X Gustav of Sweden]] (1622&ndash;1660).
* [[Maria Euphrosyne of Zweibrücken|Maria Euphrosyne]] (1625&ndash;1687); married Count [[Magnus Gabriel De la Gardie]].
* [[Countess Palatine Eleonore Catharine of Zweibrücken|Eleonore Catharine]] (1626&ndash;1692); married [[Frederick, Landgrave of Hesse-Eschwege]].
* [[Adolph John I, Count Palatine of Kleeburg|Adolf John]] (1629&ndash;1689).

