{{nihongo|'''Yasuomi Umetsu'''|梅津 泰臣|Umetsu Yasuomi|born December 19, 1960}} is a Japanese animator, director, writer, and [[storyboard artist]].

==Career==
Umetsu studied at the {{ill|Chiyoda Gakuen Educational Corporation|ja|学校法人千代田学園}} and was originally not interested in pursuing a career in the [[anime]] industry, and instead wanted to pursue a career as a [[manga]] artist.<ref name="visual">{{cite book|script-title=ja:梅津泰臣 visual art works BORDERLESS|trans-title= Yasuomi Umetsu visual art works BORDERLESS|date=October 14, 2009|publisher= Kotobukiya}}</ref> While in his 20s, he drew a short manga for [[Kodansha]] and decided to give up on pursuing manga due to feeling that it wasn't for him.<ref name="visual"/> After graduating from vocational school, he had trouble finding a job, and was introduced to [[Tsuchida Production]] through the head of the department at his school,{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} but he felt that the company's style didn't suit him and did work for other companies in the industry eventually resulting in his firing after 1–2 months.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}}

Following his brief employment with Tsuchida Production, Umetsu became involved with [[Toei Animation]] where he worked as an in-between animator and 2nd key animator for projects like ''[[Arcadia of My Youth]]'' (1982) and [[Spider-Man (1981 TV series)|the 1981 American Spider-Man]] series.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} At the studio, he worked in the same room as {{ill|Masami Suda|ja|須田正己}}, whom Umetsu learned much from in regard to key animation.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} Eventually, he was promoted to [[Animation director#Eastern production pipeline|animation director]] for ''[[Stop!! Hibari-kun!]]'' (1983);{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} however, his seniors at the company gave him a hard time since he was only 23 years old.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} When he became too uncomfortable with the environment, he moved to [[Madhouse (company)|Madhouse]], as he had an interest in working on ''[[Genma Wars|Harmagaddon]]'' (1983) due to the involvement of [[Katsuhiro Otomo]] as character designer and had been invited by [[Masao Maruyama (film producer)|Masao Maruyama]] to work with the company.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} During his time with Madhouse, he was also influenced by director [[Yoshiaki Kawajiri]], who taught him to consciously think about perspective and eye level.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=2}} He participated in ''[[Megazone 23]]'' (1985) as an animator and storyboard artist and was subsequently asked to do the original character designs for the bikers in ''Part II'' (1986), but he ended up doing the main characters for fun as well.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} He showed the president of [[Anime International Company|AIC]], Tooru Miura, and director [[Ichiro Itano]], and the two opted to use Umetsu's designs for all of the characters.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} Following this decision, Umetsu apologized to the character designer of the first OVA, [[Toshiki Hirano]].{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} Umetsu was left dissatisfied with his work on the OVA as chief animation director as much of the work was left incomplete and didn't participate as an animation director again until 1987 when he was invited by [[Hiroyuki Kitakubo]] to participate on the ''[[Robot Carnival]]'' anthology.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=1}} His contribution to the ''Robot Carnival'' anthology also marked his debut as a writer and director. Initially, Umetsu had no interest in being a director, but the idea of being able to edit every shot if needed and building his own worldview appealed to him.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}}

Following ''Robot Carnival'', however, Umetsu had no specific interest in continuing his work as a director and instead looked for more animation jobs. He described the works he took on during this time, like ''[[Grave of the Fireflies]]'' and ''[[Akira (1988 film)|Akira]]'' (1988), as being "lonely and painful", or being otherwise dissatisfied with his own work. Especially with ''Akira'', Umetsu said he felt an intense frustration that exposed his weaknesses but felt some accomplishment under the impression that director [[Katsuhiro Otomo]] was satisfied with some of the cuts he had submitted. Due to these frustrations, he gradually shifted into a directing role.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}}

Throughout the 1980s and early-to-mid 1990s, Umetsu continued to work on projects as an animator, animation director, and character designer. Two particular works were OVAs made at [[Tatsunoko Production]] and based on already-existing IPs from the studio, but he felt that director {{ill|Yukihiro Matsushita|ja|ユキヒロマツシタ}} (who was in charge of both) did not have a love for the works, so when the job for another OVA (''[[Hurricane Polymar|New Hurricane Polymar]]'', 1996) was given to him, he asked for help from [[J.C.Staff]] in producing the series and for them to lend a director.{{sfn|Hirota|2021|p=2}}{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=21}} J.C.Staff provided the project with director [[Akiyuki Shinbo]], who himself was a fan of Tatsuo Yoshida (Tatsunoko Production's founder) and other works the studio had made.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=21}} Unlike the previous OVAs, Shinbo used ''[[bande dessinée]]'' as the basis of his inspiration; and despite such a change, Umetsu stated that he thought that the team succeeded in creating the atmosphere that Shinbo wanted.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=22}} Reminiscing on the project, Shinbo noted that near the end of its production, Umetsu had once come up to him with an uncolored [[cel]] and asked him about colors, as Umetsu intended to color it himself.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=21}}

In 1995, Umetsu was involved with the ''[[Cool Devices]]'' hentai anthology under the name Touboku Akutagawa in which he created, wrote, designed the characters, and drew the storyboards for the episode titled ''Yellow Star.'' Although he was not the director of the project or extensively involved beyond the pre-production aspects, the project led to an offer to create and direct another erotic anime, which eventually became ''[[Kite (1998 film)|Kite]]'' produced by [[Arms (company)|Arms]].{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}} The film was released in several versions, but there are two prominent editions: an R-18 ''hentai'' version released in two-parts, and a single international version which removes the sexual content. Though the film has received much controversy for its sexual abuse content,<ref>{{Cite news |first=Chris |last=Beveridge |url=http://www.fandompost.com/2015/07/20/kite-blu-ray-anime-review/ |title=Kite Blu-ray Anime Review |work=Fandom Post |date=2015-07-20 }}</ref> the film has been praised as well. A writer from ''[[Anime News Network]]'' described it as a "painful, violent beauty", with note of its "gritty realism" in style and atmosphere and "expertly told" storytelling.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/review/kite|title= Kite Review|website= [[Anime News Network]]}}</ref> The film has also inspired several works in the west. For example, several scenes in the music video directed by [[Hype Williams]] for the song "[[Ex-Girlfriend (song)|Ex-Girlfriend]]" by [[No Doubt]] are based on ''Kite'';<ref name="kotaku">{{cite web|url= http://kotaku.com/5855120/brutally-graphic-adult-anime-coming-to-life/gallery/1 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20111103173508/http://kotaku.com/5855120/brutally-graphic-adult-anime-coming-to-life/gallery/1 |url-status= dead |archive-date= November 3, 2011 |title= Brutally Graphic Adult Anime Coming To Life | access-date = November 1, 2011 | date = November 1, 2011 |publisher=Kotaku }}</ref> and director-writer [[Quentin Tarantino]] recommended ''Kite'' as part of actress [[Chiaki Kuriyama]]'s preparations for her role as Gogo Yubari in the first ''[[Kill Bill]]'' (2003) film.<ref name="kotaku"/> While Umetsu continued to work freelance (not directly belonging to a studio through employment), he produced his most major works with Arms for the next decade: a second ''hentai'' OVA (also with a cut international version) titled ''[[Mezzo Forte]]'' (2000); and a sequel television series, ''[[Mezzo DSA]]'' (2004). He also contributed the character designs to ''[[Samurai Shodown|Nakoruru: Ano Hito kara no Okurimono]]'' (2002), studio Arms' first non-''hentai'' work.

Umetsu's next project was a planned film with Arms titled ''Kiss and Cry''. Issues in the development of the project halted it and a producer from the studio offered him the opportunity to create the ending animation for ''[[High School Girls]]'' (2005), which started Umetsu's popularity as a director for openings and ending for the next two decades. ''Kiss and Cry'', however, never premiered and was never officially cancelled.<ref>{{Cite web|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070711063316/http://www.accessa.co.jp/produce/kiss.html|url=http://www.accessa.co.jp/produce/kiss.html|language= Japanese|access-date= February 21, 2024|archive-date= July 11, 2007|title= KISS AND CRY}}</ref> Umetsu again tried to realize the project with a different production studio, but those plans also fell through.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}}

Although ''Kiss and Cry'' struggled to find a producer, Umetsu was asked to create a "sequel" to ''Kite'' with Arms titled ''[[Kite Liberator]]'' (2008). A single OVA was produced and sold, but the story was left unfinished. According to Umetsu, ''Kite Liberator'' the OVA fell short of its sales goal and a sequel project was not greenlit.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}} Speaking about ''Kite Liberator'' with Shinbo, Umetsu found issue with the amount of work he undertook. As the director, character designer, screenwriter, and various other production roles were all handled by him, he was constantly overworking himself.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=23}} He described ''Kite Liberator'' as "hell" to work on because of the workload, saying that he spent an entire year without taking any Sundays off and sometimes slept at the studio for five days straight.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=23–24}} In spite of the overwork, ''Kite Liberator'' is distinct in that it was Umetsu's first attempt to utilize 3DCG animation and a more contemporary style of work.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=23}} For the 3DCG in the film, the team collaborated with [[Digital Frontier]], whom Umetsu was aware of due to their work on the 2006 ''[[Death Note (2006 film)|Death Note]]'' live-action films.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=23}}

Sometime in the late 2000s, he was also offered a job as director for an original anime collaboration between [[Production I.G]] and [[Masamune Shirow]] by I.G president [[Mitsuhisa Ishikawa]]. The project was put on hold and seemingly never came to fruition, and Umetsu had few opportunities to work with Production I.G besides that. He later directed a segment of [[Dante's Inferno: An Animated Epic]] (2010) and the opening to ''[[Blood-C]]'' (2011).{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=218}}

With various projects stalled or without a greenlight, Umetsu moved onto new projects. He created a pilot film for ''[[Wizard Barristers]]'' (2014) in the early 2010s to showcase in an attempt to greenlight a series through talks with several production companies, which was difficult. During that time, [[A-1 Pictures]] producer Jouji Seita approached Umetsu for an original project since Umetsu had directed an opening for the studio's ''[[Valkyria Chronicles]]'' (2009) anime, which became ''[[Galileo Donna]]'' (2013). Both projects started production simultaneously as ''Wizard Barristers'' was able to find backing from [[Pony Canyon]].{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=219}} Umetsu tried to allocate the labor more evenly among his collaborators in ''Galilei Donna''.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=23–24}} Though he created and directed the series, and was personally involved with other aspects of it, the character designs were instead done by Shingo Adachi, and the screenplay was split between four writers. However, the series was cut down from its original two season plan to just 11 episodes.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=28}} ''Wizard Barristers'', on the other hand, was being produced by longtime collaborator Arms; however, due to production issues that delayed work, the schedule tightened which forced Umetsu to directly oversee the animation as a chief animation director, which he humored was going to kill him.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=219}} The series' delays led to much of the work in later episodes being unfinished and needing extensive revision for the Blu-Ray and DVD release.

Arms stopped producing series in 2017, and most of its staff seemingly left the company or joined its sister studio at Pierrot Plus (now [[Studio Signpost]]), with the company itself changed its name to Common Sense (keeping Arms as a trade name);<ref name=":0"/> and in 2020, the company officially filed for bankruptcy.<ref name=":0">{{cite news|url=http://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2020-08-05/ikki-tousen-elfen-lied-studio-arms-declares-bankruptcy/.162581|work=Anime News Network|title=Ikki Tousen, Elfen Lied's Studio Arms Declares Bankruptcy|last=Pineda|first=Rafael Antonio|date=2020-08-05|access-date=2022-03-14}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.tsr-net.co.jp/news/tsr/20200804_02.html|title=～テレビアニメ「一騎当千」シリーズの制作を手掛けた～}}</ref>

After his last major work in 2014, Umetsu continued to contribute mainly to openings and endings to various series and occasionally participating as a key animator for some works. In 2015, for example, Umetsu directed the opening to ''[[Gourmet Girl Graffiti]]''.<ref name="graffiti interview">{{Cite web |url=https://webnewtype.com/report/staff/57148.html|script-title=ja:新房昭之x梅津泰臣「幸腹グラフィティ」OP制作秘話|trans-title= Akiyuki Shinbo x Yasuomi Umetsu "Gourmet Girl Graffiti" OP Production Secrets|website= Newtype|date= April 1, 2015|access-date= February 22, 2024}}</ref> He also designed the characters for Pierrot's ''[[Dynamic Chord]]'' (2017) and made the character design drafts for ''[[Kokkoku]]'' (2018).<ref name="kokkoku">{{Cite web |url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2017-10-16/geno-studio-to-produce-tv-anime-of-seita-horio-kokkoku-moment-by-moment-manga/.122775|title= Geno Studio to Produce TV Anime of Seita Horio's Kokkoku: Moment by Moment Manga|website= [[Anime News Network]]|date= October 16, 2017|author= Hodgkins, Crystalyn|access-date= February 22, 2024}}</ref>

By 2017, Umetsu had a position at both Pierrot Plus (which had absorbed much of Arms' staff as its sister company) and Shaft, and was working from both locations, but by 2018 Umetsu had fully moved Shaft.{{sfn|Umetsu|2025|p=221}} Shinbo had originally asked Umetsu to direct several openings for him and the studio in the 2000s, but scheduling conflicts got in the way until he had the opportunity to direct the opening for ''[[And Yet the Town Moves]]'' (2010). The series was a precedant for Umetsu's future work with the studio as he found the company's coloring and digital photography (compositing) style to be appealing. However, as he was working on both ''Galilei Donna'' and ''Wizard Barristers'' at the time, he opted not to move forward with any project ideas. It wasn't until ''Wizard Barristers'' ended that he approached Shaft president [[Mitsutoshi Kubota]] with three ideas, of which Kubota selected one.After receiving the greenlight, he was asked to do the opening for the aforementioned ''Gourmet Girl Graffiti'' and continued contributing at other studios for the next three years. His work output halted in 2018 as his appearances, at other studios and at Shaft, diminished.{{sfn|Umetsu||2025|p=220}} He did key animation for some episodes of ''[[Magia Record]]'' (2020–2022) and was directly named by series assistant director Midori Yoshizawa as one of the contributing artists to the final episode.{{sfn|Maeda|Nakagami|Kawabata|Yonebayashi|2022|p=118}} In 2021, he directed the opening to ''[[Pretty Boy Detective Club]]'', which Shinbo said he was proud of despite the shorter production period he described it having.{{sfn|Aniplex|2021|p=14}} He participated in the Shaft-outsourced episode of ''[[The Café Terrace and its Goddesses]]'' in 2023 to support episode director Kouji Matsumura, a friend of his who was formerly at Pierrot Plus and Arms and had moved to Shaft in the mid-2010s.<ref name="umetsu terrace">{{Cite web |url=http://nailwonder.blog44.fc2.com/blog-entry-958.html|script-title=ja: 女神のカフェテラス・９話|trans-title= The Café Terrace and its Goddesses Episode 9|date= June 3, 2023|language= Japanese|access-date= January 22, 2024|website= Yasuomi Umetsu's blog|author= Umetsu, Yasuomi}}</ref> That year, he also contributed to ''[[The Quintessential Quintuplets|The Quintessential Quintuplets∽]]'' due to Matsumura directing an episode.<ref name="quintuplets umetsu">{{Cite web |url=http://nailwonder.blog44.fc2.com/blog-date-202307.html|script-title=ja:劇場公開の五等分の花嫁ＴＶＳＰ|trans-title= The Quintessential Quintuples TV Special Released in Theaters|language= Japanese|author= Umetsu, Yasuomi|website= Yasuomi Umetsu's blog|date= July 14, 2023|access-date= February 22, 2024}}</ref> Alongside [[Shouji Saeki]], Umetsu has also had a strong influence on the younger staff according to animator [[Kazuya Shiotsuki]].<ref name="artistunknown">{{Cite web |url=https://artistunknown.info/2023/08/04/interview-with-quintessential-quintuplets-tv-special-staff-at-otakon-2023/|title= Interview with Quintessential Quintuplets TV Special Staff at Otakon 2023|website= Artist Unknown|author= Nate "Kbnet|date= August 4, 2023|access-date= February 22, 2024}}</ref>

The project was finally unveiled in 2024 as a film series titled ''[[Virgin Punk]]'', with its first part slated for a summer 2025 release.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Cayanan |first1=Joanna |title=Aniplex Unveils Virgin Punk Original Animation Series by Director Yasuomi Umetsu, Studio Shaft |url=https://www.animenewsnetwork.com/news/2024-09-16/aniplex-unveils-virgin-punk-original-animation-series-by-director-yasuomi-umetsu-studio-shaft/.215594 |website=[[Anime News Network]] |access-date=September 16, 2024 |date=September 16, 2024}}</ref> According to Umetsu, the project started pre-production at the end of 2014.<ref name="umetsu blog">{{Cite web |url=http://nailwonder.blog44.fc2.com/blog-date-202409.html|script-title=ja:次回監督作品・解禁です|trans-title= My Next Directorial Work: Now Revealed|language= Japanese|website= FC2 Blog|author-last= Umetsu|author-first=Yasuomi|date= September 17, 2024|access-date= September 17, 2024}}</ref>

==Style==
Describing his own philosophies in his work, Umetsu has said that he avoids being too self-satisfying or too self-indulgent, but that he also is conscious of not making something that he's dissatisfied otherwise the "customers" (audience) to his "restaurant" (works) will be unhappy.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=24–27}} He also feels that his works have been limited due to the state of the industry. In particular, he states that there are few animators who are good at action sequences, and Umetsu's works tend to have many action sequences in them.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=24–27}} With an oversaturation of works being made and too little resources to cover them all, resources in general can be hard to come by;{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=24–27}} and in response to this, Umetsu has considered relying more on 3DCG animation and consciously making the decision to have less action scenes to make up for the lack of action animators.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|pp=24–27}}

Director Akiyuki Shinbo, himself regarded for his color style by Umetsu, has noted Umetsu's particular use of color as well.{{sfn|Shinbo|2012|p=21}}

